===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er}}

# Having a small distance from one end or edge to another, either [[horizontal]]ly or [[vertical]]ly.
#: {{syn|en|low|narrow|slim|shallow}}
#: {{ant|en|long|tall|high|broad|deep}}
# Of a person, living being{{,}} or object, having a comparatively small [[height]].
#: {{syn|en|little|pint-sized|petite|titchy<q:slang>}}
#: {{ant|en|tall}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=HarryBlank|work=w:SCP Foundation|title=Mind Over Matter|date=2022-09-22|url=https://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/8-mind-over-matter|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20240523031638/https://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/8-mind-over-matter|archivedate=23 May 2024|accessdate=24 May 2024|text=Nhung Ngo had the '''shortest''' legs at Site-43. She was the '''shortest''' member of staff, two inches beneath the positively elfin Delfina Ibanez, and yet Lillian found her inexplicably difficult to shake. Power-walking down the halls didn't do the trick, as it always did when Wettle-dodging, since the diminutive headshrink kept disappearing into commissaries or service corridors or even other people's offices and emerging, smiling, in front of her.}}
# Having little [[duration]].
#: {{syn|en|brief|concise}}
#: {{ant|en|long}}
#: {{ux|en|Our meeting was a '''short''' six minutes today. Every day for the past month it’s been at least twenty minutes long.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1980|author=Alvin Toffler|title=The Third Wave|page=230
|passage=The results of this generalized speedup of the corporate metabolism are multiple: '''shorter''' product life cycles, more leasing and renting, more frequent buying and selling, more ephemeral consumption patterns, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2012|month=March-April|author=Anna Lena Phillips|title=Sneaky Silk Moths|volume=100|issue=2|page=172|magazine=w:American Scientist|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/sneaky-silk-moths|passage=Last spring, the periodical cicadas emerged across eastern North America. Their vast numbers and '''short''' above-ground life spans inspired awe and irritation in humans—and made for good meals for birds and small mammals.|accessdate=24 May 2013|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130219220644/https://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/sneaky-silk-moths|archivedate=19 February 2013}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Kristen Rogers|title=People with ADHD have shorter life expectancy and higher risk of mental health issues, study finds|url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/01/23/health/adhd-shorter-life-expectancy-study-wellness/index.html|date=2025-01-23|passage=Having attention deficit hyperactivity disorder, or ADHD, is associated with a '''shorter''' life expectancy and a greater risk of mental health issues, according to a new study of more than 30,000 people with the disorder in the United Kingdom.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260605210949/https://www.cnn.com/2025/01/23/health/adhd-shorter-life-expectancy-study-wellness/index.html|archivedate=5 June 2026}}
